A28 — end the session when no guest image is left

Wine's own processes outlive the game: the wrapper chain that started it,
explorer and services all sit in drive_c/windows, so waiting on the primary
child alone keeps the session alive after the game quits. The reaper watches the
guest images instead — a .exe named by argv[0] or argv[1] outside wine's windows
directory — and ends the session when none is left.

A guest is usually started by path, but a launcher may start the game by name
alone ("Control_DX11.exe"), and a bare name does not say where the image lives.
Wine starts a child in the Unix directory that is its Windows current directory,
and a bare name is looked up there first, so the reaper resolves it the same way:
found in /proc/<pid>/cwd, the file's own path decides; not found, it came from
the system path and is wine's own (start.exe, winhandler.exe).

The watch is driven by events, not a poll. While a guest runs the reaper sleeps
in sigtimedwait until something happens: a child exit (SIGCHLD -- as subreaper
it inherits wine's double-forked processes, the game among them), a nudge from
the compositor (SIGUSR1, sent by A49 when a guest's window maps or goes away),
or a slow heartbeat (10 s) for an exit that produced neither. Every exited child
is reaped, not only the primary one, so a finished launcher does not stay a
zombie in the session's tree. Until the first guest is seen the table is read
every 500 ms; after the guest set empties, every 250 ms for the two seconds it
takes to call the tree still.

An empty guest set is not an exit on its own. A launcher exits before the game's
image appears, and some titles close the launcher and start a differently named
executable, so the gap can outlast any fixed window; ending there kills the game
as it starts. The reaper therefore also fingerprints the session's process tree
(every pid reachable from the primary child by parent chain) and counts idle
polls only while that fingerprint is unchanged. A handoff keeps the tree moving
— forks, exits, the successor appearing — while a finished session goes still.
An unreadable /proc leaves the flat two-second window in place.
